/**
 * @mainpage Rainwater Monitoring LoRaWAN Node
 *
 * @subpage SystemDocumentation
 *
 *
 *
 * @section Purpose Project Purpose
 *
 * The purpose of this prototype is to evaluate LoRaWAN as a
 * communication technology for monitoring rainwater buffering
 * systems used by private households.
 *
 * By monitoring stored rainwater, homeowners can:
 * - Estimate available water reserves.
 * - Track water consumption.
 * - Detect maintenance requirements.
 * - Optimize the use of harvested rainwater.
 *
 * @section MeasuredParameters Measured Parameters
 *
 * The prototype monitors three parameters:
 *
 * - Water level in the rain barrel.
 * - Water consumption through a simulated flow meter.
 * - Battery condition of the monitoring device.
 *
 *
 * @section NodeOperation Node Operation
 *
 * After power-up, the node initializes the hardware platform
 * and joins The Things Network using OTAA.
 *
 * During normal operation the node executes the following cycle:
 *
 * 1. Read all simulated sensors.
 * 2. Update indicator LEDs.
 * 3. Display diagnostic information.
 * 4. Evaluate transmission conditions.
 * 5. Build a payload when transmission is required.
 * 6. Send the payload through LoRaWAN.
 * 7. Continue monitoring.
 *
 * This cycle repeats continuously throughout the lifetime of
 * the application.
 *
 *
 * @section Hardware Hardware Components
 *
 * - HAN IoT Node
 * - LoRaWAN transceiver
 * - Prototype shield
 * - Two potentiometers
 * - One push button
 * - LEDs for visual feedback
 *
 *
 * @section SensorEmulation Sensor Emulation Method
 *
 * Physical sensors were not available during prototype
 * development. Therefore sensor behavior is emulated using
 * components available on the prototype shield.
 *
 * - The red push button emulates a flow meter pulse output.
 *   Each button press generates one pulse and increments
 *   the flow counter.
 *
 * - Potentiometer P1 emulates battery voltage.
 *   The analog input value is converted into a voltage
 *   between 0 and 5 V.
 *
 * - Potentiometer P2 emulates water level.
 *   The analog input value is scaled to represent a water
 *   level between 0 and 1.5 meters.
 *
 * This approach enables controlled testing of the software,
 * payload encoding and LoRaWAN communication functionality.
 *
 *
 *
 * @section Communication Communication Strategy
 *
 * Sensor values are periodically transmitted to
 * The Things Network (TTN) using LoRaWAN OTAA activation.
 *
 * Uplink transmissions occur:
 * - Every 5 minutes.
 * - When battery voltage reaches 4.0 V.
 * - When water level reaches 145 cm.
 *
 *
 * @section TestProcedure Prototype Demonstration
 *
 * The prototype is demonstrated using the controls available
 * on the shield.
 *
 * The demonstration procedure is:
 *
 * 1. Power the node and join The Things Network.
 * 2. Rotate potentiometer P2 to simulate changes in water level.
 * 3. Rotate potentiometer P1 to simulate battery voltage.
 * 4. Press the red push button to generate flow meter pulses.
 * 5. Observe sensor values on the serial monitor.
 * 6. Observe uplink messages in The Things Stack console.
 *
 * This demonstration verifies the complete chain from sensor
 * acquisition through payload encoding to LoRaWAN transmission.
 *
 *
 *
 * @section FutureWork Future Improvements
 *
 * - Replace simulated sensors with physical sensors.
 * - Add temperature monitoring for frost detection.
 * - Support remote configuration through LoRaWAN downlinks.
 * - Implement battery-powered operation with power-saving modes.
 * - Using the HAN IoT Shield library designed by Remko Welling
 *
 *
 * Additional information can be found in:
 * @see ShieldLayout
 * @see ApplicationArchitecture
 *
 */